  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: SQL_INSERT
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: SQL_INSERT
  language: 3
  conceptTitle: 'INSERT: Adding a Row'
  symbol: INSERT
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - SQL_05_INSERT
  - SQL_TYPE_07_INSERT
  topics:
  - INSERT
  definition: INSERT adds a new row to a table.
  howItWorks: Write INSERT INTO, the table's name and, in round brackets, the columns
    you are filling. Then write VALUES and, in round brackets, the values in the
    same order as the columns. Text values go in single quotes.
  exampleCode: "Table: pets\n id | name  | animal | age\n  1 | Bolt  | Dog    |  
    4\n  2 | Misty | Cat    |   2\n\nINSERT INTO pets (name, animal, age)\nVALUES
    ('Nemo', 'Fish', 1);"
  exampleExplanation: 'This adds a third row to pets: Nemo, a fish aged 1. ''Nemo''
    goes into name, ''Fish'' into animal and 1 into age, because the values follow
    the order of the column list.'
  tip: Many databases fill in the id column by themselves, so it is often left out.
